  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nI propose a fundamentally different approach called 'Continuous Harmonic Projection' (CHP) with Differential Evolution Optimization. Unlike the binary optimization used previously, CHP:\n   - Uses continuous harmonic projection directly onto the constraint space\n   - Models the function as a superposition of sinusoidal components\n   - Optimize not by direct descent but by harmonizing frequency components\n   - This approach exploits the underlying structure of the optimization problem to find better global solutions\n\nThe key rationale is that the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem creates a highly structured optimization landscape where harmonic projection better preserves the essential properties of the solution compared to binary discretization. Current methods are likely trapped in local optima, while harmonic projection can navigate the global structure of the solution space more effectively.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent the solution h(x) as a Fourier series with N terms:\n   h(x) = (a0/2) + \u2211_{k=1}^{N} [ak\u00b7cos(k\u00b7\u03c9\u00b7x) + bk\u00b7sin(k\u00b7\u03c9\u00b7x)]\nwhere \u03c9 = \u03c0 and dx=2.0/N. The function must satisfy:\n   \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n   0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n\nWe define the objective function as:\n   C5(h) = max_k [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) \u00b7 (1 - h(x+k)) dx] \u00b7 (2.0/N)\n\nThe optimization is performed with:\n   - Atoms = \u03d5\u2080 to \u03d5_{N-1} (harmonic basis functions)\n   - Target curve y\u2080(x) = 1/2\n   - Projection approach including entropy maximization and regularization\n   - Optimization using Differential Evolution (DE) for global search\n\nConstraints are maintained via:\n   - Projection onto the feasible set after each DE update\n   - Smart repair mechanisms using harmonic rebalancing\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nPhase 1: Setup and validation\n   - Set up N=380 grid points\n   - Project initial_h_values onto the harmonic basis\n   - Validate constraints and objective\n\nPhase 2: Harmonic initialization\n   - Create initial population of N_parameters = 2+4*(N/10)//2\n   - Each candidate is (a0, b1, b2, ..., b_{N-1}, mask_length)\n   - Initialize DE populations at multiple grid resolutions (380,500,700,800)\n\nPhase 3: Optimization loop (modeled after DE)\n   - For each candidate:\n        h = forward_transform(params)  # Fourier series reconstruction\n        fitness = C5_value(h)\n        grad = calculate_gradients(h)   # Sparse derivatives at near-discontinuities\n        params_new = mutation(params, best_neighbor_params)\n        crossover(params_new, target)\n        accept_candidate(., low_temp=True)   # Tempered acceptance\n   - Apply constraint projection using:\n        project_01(h, target_sum=190)   [0<=h<=1, \u2211h[i]*dx=1]\n\nPhase 4: Resolution adaptation\n   - If time permits, refine solution by increasing grid to 500, 700\n   - Perform 3-4 optimization steps at higher resolutions\n   - Continuously monitor elapsed time and population diversity\n\nPhase 5: Most promising candidate pruning\n   - Maintain Pareto-Front of (C5_value, solution_quality)\n   - Reinsert worse but structurally promising candidates via:\n        sub_optimization(candidate), with:\n            dx_factor=0.05, max_steps=50, step_size=10\n\nPhase 6: End-game verification\n   - Final repair and clamp to [0,1]\n   - Final orthogonalization: project along eigenroutines of autocorrelation matrix\n   - Compute final_score = C5_value(h)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n   - Population size = 50 initial, expands as diversity drops\n   - Mutation strategies: DE/binary, DE/rand-to-best, DE/current-to-best\n   - Crossover probability: 0.8 - 0.95\n   - Temperature schedule: \n        T_start = 1.0\n        T_end = 0.1\n        cooling_rate = time-based, slowing when diversity stagnates\n\nKey variations:\n   - Basis temperature control: adjust frequency components independently\n   - L1/L2 regularization strength: 0.01 - 0.1 (default 0.05)\n   - Projection frequency: every 5-10 DE generations\n   - Resolution adaptation triggers: by timer (100s per grid) and target diversity drop\n\nAlgorithm termination triggers:\n   - Time expiration (\u2264 1100s)\n   - Generation limit (N_param=800\u2192700\u2192750)\n   - Solution diversity drop below threshold\n   - Improved solution plateau\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nPrimary costs:\n   - Forward transform: O(N log N)\n   - Gradient calculation: O(2N) but very sparse\n   - DE operations: O( population * time_per_crossover)\n\nTime estimates:\n   - Low resolution 380: completes in \u2264100s\n   - Medium resolution 500: \u2264700s\n   - Full resolution 800: gets optimized only if available time\n\nFallback for early termination:\n   - Use last committed solution from running DE\n   - Retain initial_h_values if timing critically low\n   - Ensure always returning a valid solution (0<=h[i]<=1, \u2211h*dx=1)\n\nConstraint repair:\n   - Maintain scaled parameters (a,b) with known integral properties\n   - Special repair when out-of-bound: b_k = np.clip(b_k, -0.5, 0.5)\n   - Reprojection using alternating projection after DE update\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nPost-return checks:\n   validate_solution(h, N):\n        assert np.all(0 <= h[i] <= 1 for i in range(N))\n        assert np.linalg.norm(sum(h*dx) - 1) < 1e-8\n        assert C5_valuecomputed via 3 methods agrees (FFT, \n                    multiprocessing 1, \n                    multiprocessing 2)\n\nFinal score evaluation:\n   always uses: obj = max_k FFT_correlation(h) * (2.0/N)\n\nOutput:\n   (h_values, c5_bound, N) with \n        h_values: numpy array of length N\n        c5_bound: float value of minimized C5\n        N: no., of grid intervals\n\nTo detect convergence issues: \n   if solution not improving for >3 different variations:\n        trigger fallback\n\n6. Safe scaling and adaptation\nGrid transfer for given parent:\n   if parent_h is provided:\n        determine suitable harmonics for range [0,2] based on parent's cluster\n        interpolat\u011b\n        x_best = np.linspace(0,2,len(parent_h))\n        h_vals = np.interp(x_grid, x_best, parent_h)\n        then continue from there\n\nIf grid changes:\n   reproject_parametric_space(N_old, N_new) consistently\n\nThis approach may yield better long-term results by comprehending the problem as harmonization rather than search. It preserves global structure and converges faster to the global optimum.",
